; DERIVED FROM THE SPRITE ART, NOT CLONED FROM THE ISKANDER (ruling R4, 2026-09-18). This block was a
; byte-for-byte copy of mf_iskander.tres:20-26 until the frame close measured the art: mf_vauban.png
; carries 2,515 opaque pixels and EVERY ONE OF THEM IS LITERAL - not a single channel-encoded pixel -
; against the Iskander's 2522/1503/266 across channels 0/1/2. (Counted by the shader's own test:
; palette_swap_func.gdshaderinc:38-41 matches (v,0,0), (0,v,0), (0,0,v) and (0,v,v) against the seven
; red values in assets/palettes/palette_index_r.png, which are 255/221/187/153/119/85/51. Re-measured
; 2026-09-18; the ruling's own figure says 2,514, a one-pixel difference that changes nothing.)
; Every vanilla frame sprite is channel-encoded and this one is not, so the scheme cannot repaint the
; token at all: it is INERT today, whatever colours it holds.
;
; So it holds the PLACEHOLDER'S OWN colours, by a colour census of that sprite; the ruling records
; that each base is reproduced by its generated ramp's middle stop to within 2/255, which is what
; makes the slot assignment more than decorative. The point is the handover: when
; real channel-encoded art is dropped in, it inherits a default scheme that already matches what the
; placeholder looked like, instead of repainting the frame the day the art lands.
;
; SLOT ORDER IS VANILLA'S CONVENTION - most-used channel first, which is what the Iskander's own
; 2522/1503/266 spread is. base_3 is written because ColorScheme really does read it (get_color(3) ->
; target_palette_4, palette_swap_func.gdshaderinc:8); vanilla leaves it null, which on a typed Color
; export is a no-op that keeps DEFAULT_BASE.
;
; palette_2 = tone_highcontrast is UNCHANGED, and it is the Iskander's own slot and ramp: the frame
; still reads as an HA machine, it just no longer claims to be painted like one.
[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Resource_colorscheme"]
script = ExtResource("3_colorscheme")
palette_2 = ExtResource("4_toneramp")
base_0 = Color(0.29411766, 0.22745098, 0.2901961, 1)
base_1 = Color(0.8784314, 0.87058824, 0.827451, 1)
base_2 = Color(0.1882353, 0.6431373, 0.5647059, 1)
base_3 = Color(0.89411765, 0.78431374, 0.4392157, 1)

